## 1) Severity: MEDIUM | Type: Test
Title: Join the reader thread before asserting on caught exceptions

Affected:
- tests/base/src/test/java/org/keycloak/tests/admin/group/GroupTest.java:139-157

Explanation:
The background reader thread is started but never joined. Right after `deletedAll.set(true)`, the test asserts that `caughtExceptions` is empty, while the reader may still be in the middle of a request.

This has three consequences:
- An exception raised by the last in-flight request, which is exactly where a deleted group could trigger the NPE, can be added to `caughtExceptions` after the assertion has already run. The test then passes even though the bug occurred.
- The thread can outlive the test and keep calling the admin API while the next test or the realm cleanup runs. That can cause interference and flakiness.
- Only `Exception` is caught. An `Error` (for example an `AssertionError`) would silently kill the thread and never be reported.

Also note that the test cannot guarantee the race actually happens. It may pass without the fix, so it is a weak regression guard.

Proposed fix:
~~~java
Thread reader = new Thread(() -> {
    while (!deletedAll.get()) {
        try {
            managedRealm.admin().groups().groups(null, 0, Integer.MAX_VALUE, true);
        } catch (Throwable e) {
            caughtExceptions.add(e);
        }
    }
});
reader.start();

groupUuuids.forEach(id -> managedRealm.admin().groups().group(id).remove());
deletedAll.set(true);
reader.join(TimeUnit.SECONDS.toMillis(30));
assertFalse(reader.isAlive());
assertThat(caughtExceptions, Matchers.empty());
~~~

## 2) Severity: LOW | Type: Bug
Title: `getSubGroupsCount` may now return null; callers that unbox could NPE

Affected:
- model/infinispan/src/main/java/org/keycloak/models/cache/infinispan/GroupAdapter.java:274-275

Explanation:
When the group was deleted concurrently, `getSubGroupsCount()` now returns `null` instead of failing. Any caller that unboxes the returned `Long` would just move the NPE somewhere else. Examples would be `getSubGroupsCount() > 0`, assigning it to a `long`, or arithmetic on it.

I can't see the callers from this diff, so this depends on code outside it. The `GroupModel` contract also doesn't document `null` as a valid return value.

Other `GroupAdapter` methods that still go through `getGroupModel()` (for example the subgroup streams) may have the same concurrent-deletion problem. Only this one method was fixed.

Suggested steps:
- Check every caller of `getSubGroupsCount()` and make sure each one handles `null`.
- Alternatively, return `0L` for a deleted group.
- Review the sibling methods for the same issue.

Proposed fix:
~~~java
GroupModel model = modelSupplier.get();
return model == null ? 0L : model.getSubGroupsCount();
~~~
